{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "באדם", :translit "ba'adam", :gloss "in a person"} {:he "או באדם אשר יטמא לו", :translit "o ba'adam asher yitma lo", :gloss "or concerning a person who becomes impure through him"} {:he "ובוגדים באדם תוסיף", :translit "uvogedim ba'adam tosif", :gloss "and traitors against a person increase"} {:he "אשר שלט האדם באדם", :translit "asher shalat ha'adam ba'adam", :gloss "that a person rules over a person"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Masoretic note: the phrase באדם occurs here and in one other verse"} {:kind :other, :note "Juxtaposition: negaim (Lev 13–14) placed after yoledet (Lev 12)"}], :frame {:text "R. Yochanan derives from the juxtaposition of parashat ha-yoledet to parashat negaim (Shabbat 13a) that one who is not careful in the laws of niddah produces children afflicted with tzara'at. The warning against the strange woman (ishah zarah) who seduces a person appears in Proverbs 2:16 and Proverbs 7:5. The strange woman is identified in rabbinic tradition with idolatry and the evil inclination (Eruvin 21b). The metzora is considered like one who is dead — 'a metzora is considered as dead' is stated at Mo'ed Katan 9b and in the Sifra on this passage. The derash that if one improves one's deeds, God points to what He created above you — cattle and beast for your food — invokes the hierarchy of creation at Genesis 9:2–3 and contrasts it with the punishments of Leviticus 13.", :sources ["Shabbat 13a" "Proverbs 2:16" "Proverbs 7:5" "Eruvin 21b" "Mo'ed Katan 9b" "Genesis 9:2–3"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Masoretic note: the phrase באדם occurs here and in one other verse", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Juxtaposition: negaim (Lev 13–14) placed after yoledet (Lev 12)", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :leviticus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:23:08.800766231Z", :model "glm-5.2"}